<p>In 2026, the ebook landscape looks completely different than it did just two years ago. Amazon now offers DRM-free EPUB downloads for thousands of Kindle books. Kobo and Google Play have always sold DRM-free EPUBs. And a growing number of independent publishers and platforms sell unrestricted EPUB files that you actually <em>own</em> — not just license.</p>

<p>But here is the challenge most readers face: buying a DRM-free EPUB is only the first step. Without a system to organize, back up, and sync your files, you end up with a scattered collection across devices and apps. This guide walks you through the entire pipeline — from purchase to page — so you can build a DRM-free EPUB library in 2026 that you truly control.</p>

<h2>Why DRM-Free EPUBs Matter More in 2026</h2>

<p>Three major shifts have made DRM-free EPUBs the standard for ebook enthusiasts this year:</p>

<p><strong>1. Amazon went DRM-free.</strong> In December 2025, Amazon began offering DRM-free EPUB downloads for Kindle Direct Publishing titles. By mid-2026, thousands of books on the Kindle Store are available as DRM-free EPUB files. This is a tectonic shift for the largest ebook retailer on the planet.</p>

<p><strong>2. Kindle hardware is aging out.</strong> Amazon cut off store access for pre-2012 Kindles in May 2026, and the 10th-gen models lost jailbreak exploits in June. Readers are migrating to Kobo, PocketBook, and Boox devices that natively support EPUB.</p>

<p><strong>3. Authors are voting with their feet.</strong> More self-published and indie authors are choosing to sell DRM-free EPUBs directly through platforms like Kobo Writing Life, Gumroad, and Leanpub. The "Why Authors Are Leaving Amazon" trend that started in early 2026 has accelerated.</p>

<p>The result: 2026 is the year readers can finally build a genuinely DRM-free ebook library without resorting to questionable tools or workarounds.</p>

