<h2>Free and Legal DRM-Free EPUB Sources</h2>

<p>You do not need to spend money to start building your DRM-free library. These sources offer tens of thousands of free, legal EPUBs:</p>

<h3>Standard Ebooks</h3>
<p>Standard Ebooks takes public domain texts from Project Gutenberg and gives them professional-level formatting, consistent typography, and modern EPUB3 features. Every file is DRM-free, meticulously proofread, and beautifully typeset. As of June 2026, the collection includes over 900 titles.</p>

<h3>Project Gutenberg</h3>
<p>The granddaddy of free ebooks offers over 70,000 free EPUBs. Quality varies — some files have basic formatting — but for classic literature, it is unmatched. Always choose the "EPUB (with images)" option for the best reading experience.</p>

<h3>Your Local Library (Libby / OverDrive)</h3>
<p>Public library apps like Libby and OverDrive let you borrow EPUBs legally. The files are time-limited (they expire after the loan period), but you can read them on any EPUB-compatible device or app. For building a permanent library, these are a supplement, not a replacement.</p>

<h3>Internet Archive</h3>
<p>The Internet Archive offers millions of free EPUBs in its Open Library collection. Note that some in-copyright titles use Controlled Digital Lending (CDL) and behave like library loans rather than owned files.</p>

