<h2>Building Your EPUB Library with Calibre</h2>

<p>Calibre is the de facto standard for managing a DRM-free EPUB library. It is free, open-source, cross-platform, and actively maintained (version 9.10.0 was released June 25, 2026 with a new PWA content server).</p>

<p>Setting up your library takes three steps:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Install Calibre</strong> from calibre-ebook.com. The installer handles all dependencies.</li>
  <li><strong>Create a library folder</strong> on your computer or NAS. Choose a location you will back up.</li>
  <li><strong>Add your EPUBs</strong> by dragging and dropping files into the Calibre window, or use <strong>Add Books</strong> > <strong>Add from Directories</strong> for bulk import.</li>
</ol>

<p>Once your files are in Calibre, take five minutes to set up metadata:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Download metadata:</strong> Select all books, right-click, choose <em>Download Metadata and Covers</em>. Calibre fetches titles, authors, descriptions, and cover images from online sources.</li>
  <li><strong>Add custom columns</strong> for your own tags (e.g., "read," "to-read," "favorite," "reference").</li>
  <li><strong>Clean up series info:</strong> Use the metadata editor to assign series names and numbers for trilogies or series.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Organizing Your Collection for the Long Term</h2>

<p>A library of 50 EPUBs is manageable without organization. A library of 500 requires structure. Here are the organization strategies that work at scale:</p>

<h3>Virtual Libraries (Calibre)</h3>
<p>Virtual libraries act like saved searches. Create one for "Currently Reading," another for "Unread," and another for "Non-Fiction." They do not duplicate files — they are just filtered views of your master library.</p>

<h3>Tags vs. Custom Columns</h3>
<p>Tags work well for genre and mood (<code>sci-fi</code>, <code>memoir</code>, <code>page-turner</code>). Custom columns are better for metadata you want to sort or search (<code>Date Added</code>, <code>Rating</code>, <code>Source Store</code>). Use both, but keep tags under 20 per book.</p>

<h3>Folder Structure on Disk</h3>
<p>Calibre stores files in a flat author/title folder structure by default. Do <em>not</em> reorganize the Calibre folder manually — it will break the database. If you want an exportable folder structure, use Calibre's <strong>Save to Disk</strong> feature with a template like:</p>

<pre><code class="language-bash"># Calibre "Save to Disk" template for a clean folder structure
&#123;author_sort&#125;/&#123;title&#125;/&#123;title&#125; - &#123;authors&#125;
</code></pre>

<p>This gives you folders like <code>Orwell, George/1984/1984 - George Orwell.epub</code> — clean, portable, and readable by any file manager.</p>

<h2>Syncing Your Library to Every Device</h2>

<p>This is where the DRM-free advantage really shines. Because your EPUBs are unrestricted, you can use any method to get them onto any device.</p>

<h3>To Kobo (Best Experience)</h3>
<p>Kobo natively supports EPUB with the best reading experience. Use Calibre's built-in Kobo driver:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Connect your Kobo via USB.</li>
  <li>Calibre detects it and shows a device icon.</li>
  <li>Select books and click <strong>Send to Device</strong>.</li>
  <li>Calibre automatically converts to KEPUB for faster page turns and better battery life.</li>
</ol>
<p>For wireless sync, install the Calibre content server (v9.10 now includes a PWA interface) and access it from your Kobo's browser.</p>

<h3>To Kindle (Send to Kindle)</h3>
<p>Amazon's Send to Kindle service now accepts EPUB files directly. You do not need to convert to MOBI or AZW3:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Go to <a href="https://www.amazon.com/sendtokindle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">amazon.com/sendtokindle</a>.</li>
  <li>Drag your DRM-free EPUB file into the browser window.</li>
  <li>It appears on your Kindle within 60 seconds — with Whispersync, highlights, and all Kindle features.</li>
</ol>

<h3>To PocketBook and Boox</h3>
<p>Both devices treat EPUB as a first-class format. Use USB transfer, Calibre, or cloud services (Dropbox, Google Drive) to copy files. PocketBook even has built-in Dropbox sync and a "Send to PocketBook" email address.</p>

