<h2>Backing Up Your DRM-Free Library</h2>

<p>Your DRM-free EPUBs are files you actually own. Protect them like any other digital asset:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>3-2-1 rule:</strong> Three copies, two different media, one offsite.</li>
  <li><strong>Back up the Calibre folder</strong> (%APPDATA%\calibre on Windows, ~/Calibre Library on Mac/Linux). This contains both your EPUBs and your database.</li>
  <li><strong>Use cloud sync judiciously:</strong> OneDrive, Google Drive, or Dropbox work for backup, but do not let them auto-sync while Calibre is writing to the folder.</li>
  <li><strong>Export a clean folder</strong> annually using Calibre's <em>Save to Disk</em> — this gives you a portable, Calibre-independent copy.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The Complete Workflow: Purchase to Page</h2>

<p>Here is the end-to-end routine once your system is set up:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Buy or download</strong> a DRM-free EPUB from your preferred source.</li>
  <li><strong>Add to Calibre</strong> by dragging the file into the library window.</li>
  <li><strong>Let Calibre fetch metadata</strong> (title, author, cover, description).</li>
  <li><strong>Tag the book</strong> with your own categories and reading status.</li>
  <li><strong>Sync to device</strong> via USB, Calibre content server wirelessly, or Send to Kindle.</li>
  <li><strong>Read.</strong> When finished, mark it as "read" in Calibre and archive it.</li>
</ol>

<p>The entire flow takes under two minutes per book once Calibre is configured. And because every file is DRM-free, you never have to worry about which store you bought from or which device you are using today.</p>

<h2>DRM-Free EPUB Checklist for Newcomers</h2>

<p>If you are just starting your DRM-free library journey, here is what to do right now:</p>
<ol>
  <li>Install Calibre on your computer.</li>
  <li>Download 3-5 free EPUBs from Standard Ebooks to test the workflow.</li>
  <li>Set up Calibre's metadata download to auto-fetch covers and descriptions.</li>
  <li>Connect your e-reader and test the sync.</li>
  <li>Set up a cloud backup of your Calibre library folder.</li>
</ol>

